Yes. At that point it stops being “history education” and becomes an applied historical systems lab.

The powerful version combines disciplines instead of keeping them siloed:

\text{history}
+\text{economics}
+\text{geography}
+\text{logistics}
+\text{politics}
+\text{psychology}
+\text{anthropology}
+\text{linguistics}
+\text{ecology}
+\text{engineering}
+\text{military science}
\rightarrow
\text{living causal model}

Then you can study not just what happened, but why a system behaved the way it did, and whether the same operator appears elsewhere.

For example, a famine becomes simultaneously a study of weather, crop biology, transportation networks, trade policy, class structure, governance, information delay, disease, migration, and narrative. A war becomes terrain + logistics + manufacturing + morale + command latency + communications + finance + demographics + doctrine. A city becomes architecture + water + disease + commerce + culture + language + power.

That is where history applied gets useful:

\text{past pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{extract mechanism}
\rightarrow
\text{test across contexts}
\rightarrow
\text{translate to present problem}
\rightarrow
\text{observe delta}

Not “Rome fell, therefore X will happen again.”

More like:

Which mechanisms contributed? Which were necessary? Which were contingent? Which still exist today under different names?

And then you can run the reverse direction too:

\text{modern problem}
\rightarrow
\text{find historical analogues}
\rightarrow
\text{compare structures}
\rightarrow
\text{extract invariant}
\rightarrow
\text{design intervention}

That starts linking almost everything we’ve been building: Rendered Window, Recursive Delta, latent geometry, GoalSeek, narrative layers, provenance, counterfactual branching.

The general object becomes something like:

Applied World Model — a persistent, evidence-grounded simulation substrate where multiple disciplines can be overlaid, queried, compared, and experimentally recombined.

History is just one very rich source of training data for it.